    "body": "Closes #7708.\n\nCasting a ctuple to a ctuple with different item types, e.g. `<(int, int)>t` or `cython.cast((int, int), t)` with `t` being `(float, float)`, generated C code that assigned one ctuple struct to another (or passed it to the wrong to-Python converter), which fails to compile.\n\n`TypecastNode` now handles this case by casting each item separately, i.e. `<(int, int)>t` becomes `(<int>t[0], <int>t[1])`. The operand is evaluated once into a temp, so side effects are not repeated, and nested ctuples go through the same path recursively. Casting between ctuples of different sizes is now a compile-time error (\"Cannot cast '(int, int)' to '(int, int, int)'\") instead of invalid C code.\n\nThe generated code for `<(int, double)>f()` with `f()` returning `(double, int)` is now:\n\n```c\n__pyx_t_1 = __pyx_f_..._f(); ...\n__pyx_t_2.f0 = ((int)__pyx_t_1.f0);\n__pyx_t_2.f1 = ((double)__pyx_t_1.f1);\n```\n\nTests: new cases in `tests/run/ctuple.pyx` (plain cast, `cython.cast`, single evaluation of the operand, nested ctuples) and `tests/errors/ctuple_cast_size.pyx`. The run tests fail to compile without the change. I ran the C test suite locally on macOS / Python 3.13; the only failures were two end-to-end tests that need `pip` in the venv.\n",
    "closed_at": null,
